One row at the bottom, and what it costs elsewhere. Not the pronoun
— impl is that — but what having one makes possible: a verb can be
said with no noun, so the place you say it needs no width.

The row

One fixed line at the foot of a bare terminal, nothing drawn on it.
DECSTBM, ESC[1;h-1r, confines scrolling to the rows above, so the row is
written once and never scrolls off. The input is the terminal's own
cooked line, so backspace and the rest are not written a second time. No
tmux, no raw mode, no new terminal.

No bar

A title row above it was drawn, then deleted. Only two things ever fill
the middle — a view, or the last thing said — and both name themselves:
ed puts the file in its status line, fm the path in its header, c echoes
what it set. The bar repeated whichever was already on the screen.
The cost is coming back cold: c, one word, and the answer lands in the
space the discarded output left. That is the middle doing its work
instead of a row of chrome doing it forever.

A prompt is a question

z: in fm says two things — this is input, and input of what. A bare
glyph says only the first, and a fixed reserved row says that already.
So the label is not a decorated cue, it is strictly more, and it wins
wherever there is a question to ask: z:, mv:, touch:, name [x]:.
This row has none. It takes any word, so it names nothing and shows
nothing; the cursor on it is the whole signal — on the row, ready; above
it, running.
cue keeps its own place, which is neither: a question inside scrolling
output, where position is ambiguous and a prose line and a question look
the same. v asks after git status has printed. prompt, confirm and
choice are right to use it.
It comes back here if the row stops being fixed, or if the row ever has
one kind of thing to ask for.

Output is discarded

fm already works this way — spawn suspends, the child runs, Resume
clears, and the output is gone. Only a child that failed is held, by
pause, and nothing has asked for it back.
The rule underneath is addressability: output you cannot select from is
noise. What matters is file:line, and the way to keep it is to select
from it, not to scroll back to it — c `{s -n foo | fzy} consumes the
result by making it this, and the bytes are worthless after.
So the middle is not output, it is the view. And the record was never
the screen: it is m, and git.
Hold it on failure. An error that cannot be read is worse than noise.

The prompt is a row, not a process

One terminal, one keyboard: two programs cannot both read it. A row
still painted while ed owns the screen wants a multiplexer, or one
program owning everything — and `todo/address:/Not a terminal/` turned
the second down.
So the row is yielded, not owned. A key in fm and in ed hands the bottom
line to tty.ReadLine, which fm's rename already uses
(`fm/4-main/08-prompt.go`), runs the word on cur, and takes the row back.
Same row, same key, same function, same meaning — the last of those only
because they all act on one this.
Not always visible; one key away, which is what the bar was worth.
It brushes a non-goal: the readme rules out an ex line in ed. This is not
ed's command language, it is the system's, reached from inside ed — but
: is the key that reads as one, so another key keeps the difference
legible.

Modes

The split exists already and it is not command against shell, it is keys
against typed:

    the noun | pressed | typed
    cur      | fm: e   | the row: e
    said     | —       | the shell: e foo.go

The empty cell is why fm is keys: the screen is the listing, so there is
nowhere to type an argument — and with a cur there is nothing to type. A
command mode at the row is fm done twice.
And shell contains command today — the row evals, and o is a program on
$PATH that reads cur. There is no boundary to keep.
If one is ever wanted it is a prefix, not a toggle. A toggle has to be
shown, and the row that would show it was deleted for not earning
itself. ! And the rest of the line is rc, as vi's :! And everywhere else
this has been settled.

tab

The one thing that needs to know which half of the line it is in: verbs
in command position, paths in argument position.
`tty/line.go` takes the callback already; nothing passes one today.
Nothing else here is worth a mode.

To hand back

History in tty.ReadLine — a slice and two keys. The rest is there: left,
right, backspace, tab, paste, esc.
ed's status line is at the foot (`ed/4-view/07-statusline.go`), fm's header
at the head. With no frame title a viewer's own is the only title there
is, so they should agree: ed's to row 1, text from row 2.

Deferred

One program owning the screen, ed and fm as views inside it. That is
acme, and it is a window system. `todo/address:/Not a terminal/` said
no to owning the grid
and nothing since has moved the price.
